[
{ type: install
  message: <<EOM
The sysmanage agent runs as root by default.

That is deliberate.  The agent installs packages, deploys files, controls
services and reboots the host, so it needs privilege however it is deployed.

An OPTIONAL unprivileged deployment is supported.  It does not create a
security boundary -- the accompanying sudoers policy grants sudo(8) access to
install(1), chmod(1), chown(8), sed(1) and sysrc(8) with unrestricted
arguments, each of which is by itself equivalent to root.  What it does give
you is an audit trail: every privileged action is logged by sudo, whereas a
root agent logs nothing.  Choose it for accountability, not for containment.

To enable it:

  1. pkg install sudo
  2. pw groupadd sysmanage
     pw useradd sysmanage -g sysmanage -d /nonexistent -s /usr/sbin/nologin
  3. install -m 0440 -o root -g wheel \
       %%EXAMPLESDIR%%/sysmanage-agent.sudoers \
       %%PREFIX%%/etc/sudoers.d/sysmanage-agent
  4. visudo -c -f %%PREFIX%%/etc/sudoers.d/sysmanage-agent
  5. sysrc sysmanage_agent_user="sysmanage"
  6. service sysmanage-agent restart

Step 4 is not optional.  sudo rejects a file with a single malformed line by
discarding the WHOLE file, and the agent then loses every grant at once.

Review the policy before installing it.  It is a superset of what any one
site needs -- the air-gap, repository-mirroring and firewall grants are only
required if you use those features, and each can be removed.
EOM
}
]
